### Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant
* **Presenter:** Aaron Parecki
* **Slides:** [Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-oauth-sessa-identity-assertion-jwt-authorization-grant-00)
* **Draft Reference:** `draft-ietf-oauth-identity-assertion-authz-grant`
* **Updates:**
  * Added normative guidance on sender constraining (DPoP and mTLS), error responses, discovery, and SAML compatibility.
* **Discussion on Themes & Missing Building Blocks:**
  * **Just-In-Time (JIT) Provisioning:** Recommended addressing JIT user creation and claim negotiation in a separate future extension spec rather than expanding core ID-JAG.
  * **Key Binding / Proof-of-Possession:** Identified broader gaps in token exchange and JWT authorization grants regarding key binding responses and DPoP support (referencing `draft-mcguinness-oauth-jwt-grant-pop`). Recommended addressing general key binding gaps in dedicated specifications.
  * **Gateway Scenarios:** Highlighted that intermediate gateways introduce audience validation issues. Aaron Parecki and Karl McGuinness proposed bridging gateway flows using an Identity Continuation Assertion (`draft-parecki-oauth-identity-continuation-assertion`).
  * **Discovery and Multi-tenancy:** Discussed how clients indicate tenant context. Paul Carleton noted tenant configuration and discovery are broad topics best addressed in a standalone specification.
* **Next Steps:** Schedule virtual interims focused on gateway and key-binding architectures, triage core issues, and move toward Working Group Last Call for the core specification.

